WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.280
Good morning, Hank, it's Friday. It's also 117,000 degrees outside celsius, and like a boss, I decided to go

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:09.460
for a walk. Do you know what the most common irrigated crop in the United States is? Turf grass.

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:14.860
D'you know that there's three times more irrigated turf grass- y'know, I can't do this. I-I-it's TOO HOT! It is hotter

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:21.580
than Kim Jong Il... would be if he died and went to hell. Uuuuuugghhh. Yet more evidence, Hank, that outside is

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:25.800
overrated. Right, so there's three times more irrigated turf grass in the United States than there is corn,

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:33.440
but more importantly, almost ALL of the water that we irrigate turf grass with is potable. About 30% of the overall

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:39.660
drinking water supply in America goes to feed grass. Hank, it seems to me that the American front lawn is the

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:46.540
best possible example of what is called status quo bias can lead to, like, COMPLETE INSANITY. I mean, obviously

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:54.080
front lawns are a TERRIBLE idea. I mean, Hank, what do all of these front lawns have in common? NO ONE IS

00:00:54.080 --> 00:01:00.340
USING THEM! They're just sitting there, sucking 4 billion gallons of drinking water every frackin' day! Also, in order

00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:07.600
to maintain this completely unnatural plant monoculture, we use 70 million pounds of pesticides every year on

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:14.480
our grass. Grass which, I will remind you, DOES NOT FEED US! I'm sorry, Hank, but it is very hot, and just like

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:20.660
characters in Victorian novels, the temperature reflects my temperament! 70 million pounds of pesticides a year.

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:25.580
It's ridiculous! And the most ridiculous part is that I'm out there with them, trying to make sure that my grass looks

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:29.660
okay so my neighbors don't get mad at me. Now, Hank, there are some advantages to lawns. Grass turns carbon

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:33.780
dioxide into oxygen, although it's hardly the most efficient carbon sink, and there's some soil erosion

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:38.420
benefits, although we'd all be better off if we had, like, a big mix of plants on our front yard, and we'd be better off

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:44.660
STILL, if we had vegetable gardens! So how did we end up with the disaster that is the front lawn? The British.

00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:50.140
That's right, Hank, we threw off their colonial yoke and said no to taxation without representation, but they got

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:55.700
us on the lawns! The lawn is a British invention, but the idea isn't as crazy in Britain because, as you know, it

00:01:55.700 --> 00:02:00.700
rains all the freaking time. So they don't have to pump 4 billion gallons of drinking water every day into their

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:05.220
lawns. On the downside, they never get to see the sun. Also, James Murdoch. So, Hank, we're all biased toward

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:09.680
the status quo. Not to beat a dead horse, but that's the only reason we still have pennies. Like, if someone tried

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:13.740
to introduce a coin that was worth a thousandth of a dollar today, people would be, like, 'that's insane!'

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:18.420
Well, it's equally insane to have a coin that's worth a hundredth of a dollar, but since it already exists, we're

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:23.040
all okay with it. So Hank, if you can close your eyes and imagine a world in which there's a huge vegetable

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:29.020
garden in the front of every home in suburban America, and there's a multi-billion dollar industry of people taking

00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:33.860
care of these vegetable gardens, and watering them, and helping you to harvest the crops. Your food costs go

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:38.660
down, you have more vegetables in your diet, we use less oil and gas to transport food. But then one day, you

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:43.520
decide to rip up your vegetable garden and become the first person in your neighbourhood to lay down turf

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:47.820
grass. Your neighbours are gonna come over and be like, "hey, what's the new thing?" And you'll be like, "that's turf

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:52.220
grass." And they'll be like, "great! Uh, what do you cook it with?" And you'll be like, "no, you don't eat it." In short,

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:55.580
Hank, your neighbours are gonna think you're crazy, partly because they have status quo bias toward this

00:02:55.580 --> 00:03:00.660
world in which all front yards are composed of vegetable gardens, and partly because... they're right. But Hank, the

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:05.540
weirdest thing about status quo bias is that it doesn't actually preserve the status quo. Over the last forty

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:10.740
years, American lawns have gotten bigger and bigger and bigger and bigger. We're trying to preserve the status

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:16.320
quo and we have an idea in our head that's fueled by advertising that lawns should be massive. I know this,

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:20.300
Hank, because it happened to me. When we moved to Indianapolis, I was like, "Sarah, we gotta get a HUGE

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:24.860
YARD, I want a riding lawn mower," and she was like, "you are not going to enjoy mowing the lawn," and I was like,

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:30.980
"I'm gonna LOVE moving the lawn!" Yeah, so long story short, the heat index is 114 degrees, and I have to go

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:33.246
mow the lawn. Hank, I''ll see you on Monday.

00:03:33.246 --> 00:03:33.746
 

